sh·cloud·GH#27730066457", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Alfonso IV, called the Monk, was King of León from 925 and King of Galicia from 929, until he abdicated in 931.", "newValue": "Alfonso IV, known as 'the Monk', ruled the Kingdom of León from 925 to 931, following a complex succession dispute after the death of his brother Ordoño II. His brief reign was characterized by internal instability, military engagements against the expanding Caliphate of Córdoba, and a dramatic abdication that profoundly influenced the Leonese succession. His decision to retire to a monastery, only to later reclaim his throne, underscores the volatile political landscape of early 10th-century Iberia and the intertwined roles of secular power and religious devotion in the Reconquista era.

Born in 899 CE, Alfonso ascended to the throne of León in 925 after the death of his childless brother, Fruela II, and a power struggle with his cousins, the sons of Ordoño II. He secured the crown of Galicia in 929, unifying significant portions of the northwest Iberian peninsula under his rule. Despite these political consolidations, his reign was marked by continued pressure from Abd al-Rahman III's Caliphate of Córdoba, particularly along the Douro frontier. In 931, Alfonso, seemingly moved by religious conviction or perhaps political weariness, abdicated his throne in favor of his brother Ramiro II and entered the monastery of Sahagún.

